Track holders and whether an address was EVER held, and record that this is now too heavy.

Three additions to the #227 instrument, and the last of them is the finding.

WHAT THEY ADD:
  ever-held   a never cleared set, because "not registered" was ambiguous: an address may never
              have been stored in a catalog entry, or stored and then forgotten when a holder
              deallocated. Different bugs.
  holders     the ring now records WHICH catalog colour stored each colour, so forgetting is per
              holder rather than per colour. Clearing every entry that shared an address made a
              colour still held by somebody else report "not registered".
  short       at catalog dealloc, print only when the retain count CANNOT cover the live holders,
              which is the anomaly and nothing else.

WHAT THEY MEASURED, on a death:
  self-class=NSColor_catalog        the receiver is live and correctly classed
  ever-held=YES                     the address WAS a registered colour at some point
  live-holders=0                    no live entry in the ring holds that address
  still-registered=no  evicted=0 of 46
  COLORLIFE SHORT                   never fired: no retain count was ever short of its holders

AND THE FINDING: THE INSTRUMENT NOW SUPPRESSES THE BUG. 0 of 16 with this in, against 2 of 8 and
1 of 2 with the lighter version, and 5 of 16 with none. That is the FIFTH perturbation to make it
vanish, after a colour trace, zombies, an extra retain and this. The comparison this patch was
built to print, what a holder REGISTERED against what its _color reads now, cannot be obtained
from an instrument that stops the failure happening.

SO THE FORK IS STILL OPEN, and I have corrected myself twice on it. The object died under an
intact field, or the field was overwritten with another entry colour pointer; ever-held=YES fits
BOTH and settles nothing, which is the opposite of what I said when I first read it.

The next approach must not be another probe in this path. Something outside it: a guarded
allocator, or a reduction to a small program that provokes the same thing without Qt.

